      "ballot_id": "B002",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Hidetoshi Katori",
        "Jun Ye"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Optical lattice clocks combine exceptional precision with a new level of control over atomic reference standards, enabling sensitive tests of fundamental physics as well as important measurement applications.",
        "attribution": "Katori and Ye independently established leading approaches to lattice clocks and have both driven the field; credit for subsequent technical advances is broad.",
        "maturity": "The clocks have been developed and compared across laboratories over many years, with precision and reproducibility now firmly established.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The extent to which the prize should recognize the clock concept itself versus later advances in precision and applications needs careful delimitation."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 2,
      "ballot_id": "B012",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Andrew N. Cleland",
        "Markus Aspelmeyer",
        "Tobias J. Kippenberg"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Quantum control of mechanical motion directly probes how quantum behavior persists in increasingly massive systems, a deep question about the boundary between quantum and classical physics.",
        "attribution": "These researchers made complementary contributions to controlling, cooling, and measuring mechanical systems in the quantum regime, though the field also rests on a wider experimental and theoretical community.",
        "maturity": "Quantum signatures and control have been demonstrated repeatedly with several platforms, while more ambitious tests of macroscopic quantum behavior remain in progress.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The discovery is a sequence of milestones rather than one sharply bounded result, so the prize contribution and attribution would need precise definition."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 3,
      "ballot_id": "B001",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Michael V. Berry"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Geometric quantum phases revealed that quantum evolution can carry observable phase information tied to the path through parameter space, with lasting consequences across atomic, molecular, and condensed-matter physics.",
        "attribution": "Berry's formulation of the adiabatic phase was influential and distinct; Aharonov's work on quantum phases is also foundational, though the precise scope of the joint discovery needs care.",
        "maturity": "The geometric phase has been observed and used in many systems for decades, with extensive experimental confirmation.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The ballot combines related but historically distinct contributions, including the earlier Aharonov–Bohm effect, so the discovery being honored must be stated narrowly."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 4,
      "ballot_id": "B028",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Juan Ignacio Cirac",
        "Peter Zoller",
        "Rainer Blatt"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Trapped ions made controlled quantum logic experimentally concrete and helped establish the physical principles of quantum computation, while also enabling precision studies of quantum systems.",
        "attribution": "Cirac and Zoller provided central theoretical proposals, and Blatt's group demonstrated key operations experimentally; their complementary roles support this attribution.",
        "maturity": "Ion control and entangling operations have been reproduced and extended across laboratories, although scalable fault-tolerant computation remains an active challenge.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "Quantum computation is still developing, and it is not yet clear which early milestones best define the lasting physical discovery."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 5,
      "ballot_id": "B023",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Charles L. Kane",
        "Eugene J. Mele",
        "Laurens W. Molenkamp"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "The prediction and experimental realization of quantum spin Hall states and topological insulators opened a broad new area of quantum materials and demonstrated robust phases distinguished by topology.",
        "attribution": "Kane and Mele developed key theoretical predictions, and Molenkamp's experiments established the quantum spin Hall effect in a material system; earlier theoretical foundations also matter.",
        "maturity": "The phenomena have been confirmed in multiple materials and have generated sustained theoretical and experimental work.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The candidate description spans several related developments, and credit could reasonably extend to earlier work on topological phases."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 6,
      "ballot_id": "B004",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Michel Della Negra",
        "Peter Jenni",
        "Tejinder S. Virdee"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "The ATLAS and CMS discovery of the Higgs boson confirmed a central prediction of particle physics and completed a major part of the Standard Model.",
        "attribution": "The discovery was made by two large collaborations; these names represent leadership in building the experiments, but the result depended on thousands of contributors.",
        "maturity": "The signal was independently observed by both experiments and its properties have since been studied extensively.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The individual laureates would represent collaboration-wide work, raising difficult questions about how fairly a small prize can convey the achievement."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 7,
      "ballot_id": "B047",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Christophe Salomon",
        "John E. Thomas",
        "Martin Zwierlein"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Strongly interacting Fermi gases provide a clean, tunable setting for studying universal quantum many-body behavior and connections to superconductivity and nuclear matter.",
        "attribution": "The work spans several pioneering experimental groups and complementary regimes; these three represent substantial contributions, but the proposed credit is not exhaustive.",
        "maturity": "Experiments have produced a rich and reproducible body of results over many years, although important questions about strongly correlated fermions remain open.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The discovery is a broad research program with multiple pioneers, making a three-person attribution particularly difficult.",
          "Some of the field's most consequential physical conclusions remain under active investigation."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 8,
      "ballot_id": "B030",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Antoine Browaeys",
        "Mark Saffman",
        "Mikhail D. Lukin"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Programmable Rydberg arrays offer unusually direct control of interacting atoms and have enabled new studies of quantum many-body dynamics and information processing.",
        "attribution": "Browaeys, Saffman, and Lukin have each made important contributions to the experimental platforms and their development, alongside substantial work by other groups.",
        "maturity": "The systems have produced striking and increasingly reproducible results, but the area is young and its lasting conceptual impact is still taking shape.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "It is too early to know which results from programmable arrays will become the field's defining contribution.",
          "The proposed discovery combines platform development with applications that are still evolving."
        ]
      }
    }
  ]
